  "title": "Preserve selected circle corners through replay and repeated offsets",
  "owner": "Hypercurve common point fields, selected-circle incidence and endpoint transport, corner reconstruction, arrangement side rays, finite projection and native circle definitions",
  "domain": "The named jointly selected degree-seven/five circle centers, represented metric frames, signed radii, translated support contacts, finite and reversed overlaps, and diameter-corner edits with explicit algebraic constraints.",
  "proof": "Common-field relations retain exact translated circle/circle and nonincident circle/line contacts, original radius equations, finite domains and tangent signs. Derived-point query views preserve coordinate guards and source identity. Coincident supports reuse the existing angular correspondence authority; overlap endpoints replay through their original point definitions. Mapped cuts use the fragment endpoint authority after concentric offsets. A finite set of distinct side rays in the certified normal half-plane avoids endpoint-collinear candidates without guessing winding. Exact point translations retain consumed policy and inherited barriers. Projection reuses only vertices whose exact connectivity is validated, while native definitions remain authoritative.",
  "witness": "The unchanged chamfer and constrained fillet programs now complete construction, independent contact checks, boundary reconstruction, path and filled-region replay, round dilation/contraction and semantic XOR. Twelve additional public fixtures cover exact roots, tangency, finite clipping, 2^-80 gaps, selected halves, reversal, partial overlap restriction/replay, fresh-process contact replay, policy transitions and resource retry. Three unit regressions cover mapped cut rays and retained policy dependencies. A CSGRS adapter fixture constructs a fillet from a compact exact seed, reloads its filled result and consumes further offsets.",
  "gaps": "Six fresh probes on this source still fail whole empty-document export or inherited empty-topology replay/projection. Their source-attributed programs are archived as a separate follow-on frontier. This is a bounded completion envelope for the named shared-field circles and corner chains. General independent coefficient fields, nonrepresented circle frames, arbitrary algebraic curve operations, complete cancellation and total arithmetic/memory bounds remain open.",
